ademy/question-capture/13981249/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981249/state.json" "file_sha256" "aabaa3971ab09c99ebf065fdd4173e7fe235b0f15d5ccee8e707b7d7b9e3445c" "basis" 884 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981249/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={70}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {70}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) & =2\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981249/state.json" "file_sha256" "aabaa3971ab09c99ebf065fdd4173e7fe235b0f15d5ccee8e707b7d7b9e3445c" "basis" 884 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981249/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-120088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/q